.TH TWIST 1 "2026" "twist 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
twist \- transfer a URL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B twist
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fIURL\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B twist
transfers each \fIURL\fR.
The body is written to standard output, or to the file named by
.B \-o
or
.BR \-O .
Diagnostics are written to standard error, or to
.BR \-\-stderr .
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-version
are written to standard output and exit 0.
A transfer error has the form
.B twist: (\fIN\fP) \fImessage\fP
and exits
.IR N ,
a status from the table under
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
A password, token or passphrase given as an argument
.RB ( \-u ,
.BR \-U ,
.B \-\-oauth2\-bearer
and
.BR \-\-pass )
is blanked in the list of arguments that
.BR ps (1)
shows once twist has taken it, as curl's is.  A cookie given with
.BR \-b ,
and a header given with
.BR \-H ,
are not.
A bad command line exits 2, with no number in parentheses, and a line
that suggests
.BR \-\-help .
.B \-s
hides a transfer diagnostic.
.B \-S
shows it again.
Several URLs leave the status of the last transfer, unless
.B \-\-fail\-early
stops at the first failure, or
.B \-Z
is given (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
.PP
The schemes that are implemented are listed under
.BR PROTOCOLS .
.SH PROTOCOLS
A URL scheme selects the protocol.  The scheme with an \fBs\fR is the same protocol inside TLS from the first byte.  \fBftp\fR, \fBimap\fR, \fBpop3\fR and \fBsmtp\fR can be upgraded to TLS after they start, when \fB\-\-ssl\fR or \fB\-\-ssl\-reqd\fR asks; nothing else is.
.TP
.B dict
Looks up a word with a DICT server: DEFINE, MATCH or SHOW, chosen by the URL path.  The reply is written until the server closes.
.TP
.B file
Reads the local file named by the URL path.  The path is percent-decoded, and a
NUL in it is a malformed URL (exit 3).  The host is empty, localhost or
127.0.0.1, and any other exits 3.  Only a regular file is read: a FIFO, a
device or a directory is refused with exit 37, and is not waited on.
.B \-r
takes part of the file.
.TP
.B ftp, ftps
Downloads a file, or lists a directory when the URL path ends in a slash.  \fBftps\fR is TLS from the first byte (port 990), and its data connection is protected too (PBSZ 0 and PROT P), resuming the session of the control connection; a server that will not protect it ends the transfer with status 35.  \fBftp\fR is upgraded with AUTH TLS only when \fB\-\-ssl\fR or \fB\-\-ssl\-reqd\fR asks, and its data connection is then protected the same way.
.TP
.B gopher, gophers
Sends the selector from the URL and writes the reply until the server closes.
.TP
.B http, https
HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, as the \fB\-\-http*\fR options and the server allow.  Over HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 the header names are sent in lower case, and the fields that belong to an HTTP/1 connection are left out of the request, as RFC 9113 section 8.2.2 asks: Connection, Keep\-Alive, Proxy\-Connection, Transfer\-Encoding, Upgrade, and TE unless its value is \fItrailers\fR.  A header of \fB\-H\fR that is one of them is not an error.  \fB\-\-unix\-socket\fR and \fB\-\-abstract\-unix\-socket\fR send the request over a Unix socket.
.TP
.B imap, imaps
Fetches a message from a mailbox, or lists the mailboxes.
.TP
.B ldap, ldaps
Binds and searches, and writes the result as LDIF.
.TP
.B mqtt, mqtts
Subscribes to the topic in the URL path, or publishes \fB\-\-data\fR and disconnects.
.TP
.B pop3, pop3s
Retrieves a message, or lists the messages in the maildrop.
.TP
.B scp, sftp
Both run over SFTP on an SSH connection: a file up or down, and, for \fBsftp\fR, a directory listing.  There is no SCP wire protocol.  SSH provides the encryption, so there is no TLS variant.
.TP
.B smtp, smtps
Sends a message to the server, from \fB\-\-mail\-from\fR to each \fB\-\-mail\-rcpt\fR.
.TP
.B telnet
A telnet session: the server's text is written to the output and standard input is sent.  See \fBTELNET\fR.
.TP
.B tftp
Reads a file, or uploads one with \fB\-\-data\fR or \fB\-T\fR.  It is a datagram protocol: no proxy is used.
.TP
.B ws, wss
WebSocket, over HTTP/1, HTTP/2 or HTTP/3.  The payload bytes are written.
.PP
The schemes with a trailing s are TLS from the first byte.  The others are not
upgraded unless asked: only ftp, imap, pop3 and smtp can be, with
.B \-\-ssl
or
.BR \-\-ssl\-reqd ;
ldap and mqtt cannot.  A user name and password given for any of these, and not
upgraded, go on a connection that is not encrypted, by a method such as PLAIN,
LOGIN or a simple bind that does not hide them; PLAIN is tried when the server
advertises none.  Use the scheme with the s, or
.BR \-\-ssl\-reqd .
.SH "EXPLICIT TLS"
.B \-\-ssl
and
.B \-\-ssl\-reqd
upgrade a connection that starts in the clear, for ftp, imap, pop3 and smtp,
with the command each protocol has: AUTH TLS (RFC 4217), STARTTLS (RFC 3501 and
RFC 3207) and STLS (RFC 2595).  They do nothing for a scheme that is TLS
already, and nothing for the others.  Without one of them twist never asks,
however much the server offers.
.PP
The upgrade comes first.  twist reads what the server offers and asks for TLS,
and the user name and password are sent only after the handshake, which checks
the certificate as for any other TLS connection
.RB ( \-\-cacert ,
.BR \-k ).
What the server said before the handshake is forgotten, and its capabilities
are asked for again.  With ftp the data connection is then protected as on
ftps, with PBSZ 0 and PROT P, and a server that will not ends the transfer.  A
server of imap or pop3 that has no capability list cannot be asked whether it
offers the upgrade, and counts as not offering it.
.PP
.B \-\-ssl\-reqd
ends the transfer with exit status 64 if the server does not offer the upgrade
or refuses it, and no password has been sent.
.B \-\-ssl
goes on in the clear instead.  That is as secure as
.B \-\-ssl\-reqd
when the server agrees and not otherwise, and whoever is on the path can make a
server seem not to offer TLS, so it protects against nobody who is.  Use
.BR \-\-ssl\-reqd .
.PP
A server that has said yes and then sends anything before the handshake, in the
same packet or after it, has sent it in the clear, from anyone on the path, and
it is not taken as part of the encrypted session: the transfer ends with exit
status 8.
.PP
These schemes are recognised, but are not implemented, and exit 1:
.B rtmp, rtmps,
.B rtsp,
.B smb, smbs.
.SH TELNET
.B telnet://host[:port]/
(port 23) opens a connection and runs a telnet session on it (RFC 854): what the
server sends is written to the output, and what is read from standard input is
sent to the server.  The path of the URL is not used.  The session ends when
the server closes the connection, with exit status 0, or at the limit of
.BR \-m .
.PP
What is sent is standard input, which is read as it comes, so that a terminal,
a pipe or a file may be used and what is typed goes to the server at once; or
the file of
.BR \-T ,
and
.B \-T \-
is standard input.  Each byte of 255 is sent twice, as the protocol needs.
Nothing else is added or changed: a line ends as it does in the input.  The
data of
.B \-d
is not sent.  The end of the input does not end the session, which is the
server's to end.  A transfer that is much behind the connection waits for it,
and does not hold the input in memory.
.PP
What is written is the server's text without its commands.  A carriage return
followed by a NUL is a carriage return, and 255 twice is a byte of 255.  A
command that twist does not know is dropped.
.PP
twist negotiates with a server that negotiates, and only then; to one that does
not, it speaks plain text, so that a telnet URL may be used to talk to a mail
or an echo service.  The first time the server sends a command (WILL, WON'T,
DO or DON'T), twist answers it and asks for the options it wants: binary
transmission and suppress go\-ahead, both ways.  It accepts the server's echo,
but does not ask for it.  Every other option the server offers or asks for is
refused, except the terminal type, the X display location and the environment,
which a server that asks with a sub\-negotiation is told the values of, and the
window size, which it is told if it asks.
.PP
.B \-t
takes
.IB name = value\fR,
with the name in either case:
.TP
.BI TTYPE= type
The terminal type, such as vt100, up to 31 bytes.
.TP
.BI XDISPLOC= display
The X display, such as host:0, up to 127 bytes.
.TP
.BI NEW_ENV= name , value
An environment variable.  Give the option once for each.
.B NEW_ENV=\fIname\fR
is a variable with no value.
.TP
.BI WS= width x height
The size of the window in characters, each from 1 to 65535.  Without it a server
that asks is told 0 by 0, which means that it may guess.
.TP
.BI BINARY= n
1, the default, asks for binary transmission; any other number does not.
.PP
Giving one of the first four offers it, with the other options twist wants,
when the negotiation starts.  The user name of
.B \-u
or of the URL is sent as the variable USER, which offers the environment too.
A value with a byte above 127 is skipped without an error; a user name with one
is refused (exit status 43).  A name that is not one of these is exit status
48, and an option with no = or a bad value is exit status 49, both before twist
connects.  There is no TLS variant,
.B \-\-limit\-rate
is refused, and the size of a terminal is not found out and sent: use WS.  A
proxy can be used, and the session goes through its tunnel.
.SH URLS
A host is a name made of letters, digits and the characters of a name, with a
dot, a hyphen or an underscore, or an IPv4 address, or an IPv6 address in
brackets.  A name in UTF-8 is taken.  A space or control character, a
backslash, a percent sign, or one of
.B | ^ ` < > " { }
in the host, a port that is not only digits or is above 65535, and anything
after the closing bracket of an IPv6 address other than a port, make a URL
malformed: exit status 3.  An empty port, as in
.IR http://host:/ ,
is the default one.
.PP
The path and the query of a request are sent percent-encoded: a space or a byte
above 0x7f is written
.BI % XX \fR,
and an escape that is there already is not encoded again.
.SH "PROGRESS METER"
Twist shows the progress of a transfer on standard error, or on
.BR \-\-stderr :
a heading of two lines and then one line that is written over
and over, with the amount transferred, the speeds, and the time spent and
left.  The sizes are in bytes, and with the suffixes k, M, G, T and P as
multiples of 1024.  The columns are the percentage and size of the whole
transfer, the percentage and amount received, the percentage and amount sent,
the average download and upload speeds, the time the transfer will take, the
time it has taken and the time it has left, and the speed over the last few
seconds.  Where a size is not known, the percentages are 0 and the times
\fB\-\-:\-\-:\-\-\fR.
The line is drawn when the second changes, after a second with nothing to
draw, and once more at the end, and the end of the line is a newline.
.PP
The meter is not shown when the body is written to a terminal, since it would
mix with it: a download, or an upload to HTTP, with no
.B \-o
or
.BR \-O ,
on a terminal.  It is not shown with
.B \-s
or
.BR \-\-no\-progress\-meter .
.B \-#
draws a bar instead, a line of # signs across the screen and the percentage
when the size is known, and when it is not a spaceship with hash signs flying
above it.
The width is that of the terminal, or the environment variable
.BR COLUMNS ,
if that is a number from 21 to 9999, and 79 if neither is known.
The bar ends its line after any error message.
.PP
A size is known for the body of an HTTP response that has Content-Length, a
file, an FTP download whose server says how big it is, an sftp or scp
download, an IMAP message, and the data sent with
.BR \-d ,
.B \-T
or an SMTP, MQTT, TFTP or sftp upload.  A redirect finishes the line, and the
next response starts again at 0.
.PP
With
.B \-Z
the transfers share one line for the whole run, on standard error or
.BR \-\-stderr ,
under a heading that is printed once:
.PP
.nf
DL% UL%  Dled  Uled  Xfers  Live Total     Current  Left    Speed
 62 --   2500     0     8     3   0:00:01  0:00:01 --:--:--  2500
.fi
.PP
The line begins with a carriage return and is written over.  The columns are
the percentage of the download that is done and of the upload; the bytes
downloaded and uploaded; the number of transfers started so far, and of those
that are under way; the time the whole download will take, the time spent and
the time left; and the speed, which is the higher of the download and the
upload over the last ten lines drawn, or since the start before there have
been ten.  A percentage is shown only when every transfer under way has a
size, and the sizes of all of them, those that are over too, add up to more
than 0; otherwise it is
.BR \-\- .
A transfer that has ended stays in the bytes and the sizes.  The times are
\fB\-\-:\-\-:\-\-\fR when the download size is not known for every transfer
under way, or nothing has moved.  A line is drawn when half a second has
passed since the last, and once more at the end, which ends with a newline.
A message about a transfer that failed is written after the carriage return,
as it comes, and not kept apart from the line.
.PP
The line is not shown with
.B \-s
or
.BR \-\-no\-progress\-meter ,
nor when the body of any URL goes to a terminal (a URL with no
.B \-o
or
.BR \-O ),
since it would mix with it.
.B \-#
is ignored: the line is drawn and not the bar.
.SH VARIABLES
A command line can set variables and use them in the parameters of options.
.TP
.BI \-\-variable " name=text"
.TQ
.BI \-\-variable " name@file"
Set a variable.
The name is letters, digits and underscores.
A file of
.B \-
is standard input.
The content can be anything, up to ten megabytes.
.TP
.BI \-\-variable " %name"
.TQ
.BI \-\-variable " %name=text"
Import the environment's variable.
Without
.B =text
it is an error if the environment does not have it; with it, that is the
value when it does not.
.TP
.BI \-\-expand\- option " parameter"
Any option that takes a parameter, with each
.B {{name}}
in the parameter replaced by the variable's content, empty if there is none.
.B \\{{
is
.BR {{ .
The URL given as an argument is not expanded; use
.B \-\-url
for one that is.
Content with a NUL in it is an error unless a function encodes it.
.PP
Functions follow the name after colons and apply from left to right:
.BR {{name:trim:b64}} .
.B trim
removes white space from both ends,
.B json
quotes the content for the inside of a JSON string,
.B url
percent-encodes it,
.B b64
encodes it as base64, and
.B 64dec
decodes base64.
.PP
.nf
twist --variable name=smith --expand-url 'https://example.com/{{name}}'
.fi
.SH "URL GLOBBING"
A URL may name many things at once, for any scheme.
.TP
.BI { a,b,c }
One of these, in turn.  A set cannot hold a set, and a backslash before a
character in it makes it plain.
.TP
.BI [ 1-10 ]
A range of numbers.
.B [01-10]
keeps the width of its start, and
.B [1-10:3]
takes every third.
.TP
.BI [ a-z ]
A range of letters, in one case, which also takes a step.
.PP
A URL with several of these makes all their combinations, the last going
fastest, and a very large number of them is no trouble: they are made one at a
time.
A backslash before a brace or a bracket makes it plain text, and an IPv6
address in brackets is left alone.
A pattern that is wrong exits 3 with a message that shows where.
.PP
In the name given to
.B \-o
the text
.B #1
is what the first glob was in the URL, and
.BR #2 ,
the second, and so on; a number with no glob stays as it is.
An
.B \-o
goes with the URL it is next to, and so with all that URL makes, so a name with
no
.B #
is written over by each.
.B \-O
names each from its own URL.
.B \-g
turns all of this off.
.PP
.nf
twist -o 'page_#1.html' 'https://example.com/p[1-3].html'
twist -O 'sftp://host/{a,b,c}.txt'
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a help message on standard output and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and license information on standard output and exit
successfully.  A
.B Features:
line names the optional libraries that twist was built with,
.B brotli
for libbrotli.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " file"
Write the body to \fIfile\fR.
.B \-
is standard output.
.TP
.B \-O, \-\-remote\-name
Write the body to the last segment of the URL path.  A last segment of
.I \-
is a file called
.IR \- ,
not standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " dir"
Directory for a relative
.B \-o
or
.B \-O
name.  Reset by
.BR \-\-next .
.TP
.B \-\-create\-dirs
Create missing parent directories for
.B \-o
and
.BR \-O ,
with mode 0750 whatever the umask is.  Reset by
.BR \-\-next .
.TP
.BI \-X ", " \-\-request " method"
Request method.
.TP
.BI \-H ", " \-\-header " line"
Extra request header.  Repeat for each header.
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-data " data"
Request body.  A leading
.B @
reads a file, and
.B @\-
reads standard input.  Carriage returns and line feeds are removed.
Repeated
.B \-d
values are joined with
.BR & .
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-binary " data"
Request body, kept as given, including a file read with
.BR @ .
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-raw " data"
Request body.
.B @
is not a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-urlencode " data"
Form field.  The value is percent-encoded.
.B name=value
encodes the value,
.B name@file
encodes the file, and
.B @file
encodes the file with no name.
.TP
.B \-G, \-\-get
Append
.B \-d
to the query string instead of sending a request body.
.TP
.BI \-T ", " \-\-upload\-file " file"
Upload \fIfile\fR with PUT.
.B \-
reads standard input.  The file is read before the transfer starts.  A telnet
session is not a PUT: it sends the file as it is read, and
.B \-
is what it reads without
.BR \-T ,
standard input.
.TP
.BI \-t ", " \-\-telnet\-option " name=value"
An option of a telnet session: TTYPE, XDISPLOC, NEW_ENV, WS or BINARY.  Give
the option as many times as needed.  See
.BR TELNET .
Another scheme ignores it.
.TP
.B \-I, \-\-head
Fetch headers only.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-include
Write response headers before the body.
.TP
.BI \-D ", " \-\-dump\-header " file"
Write response headers to \fIfile\fR.
.B \-
is standard output.
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-location
Follow redirections, up to
.B \-\-max\-redirs
of them.  A redirect goes only to http, https, ws or wss.  One to another
scheme exits 1, and one to a URL that names no host, such as
.IR http:// ,
exits 3.  The Location is resolved against the URL as RFC 3986 section 5.2
says.  A 301, 302 or 303 is followed with GET and no body, and a 307 or 308
keeps the method.  A redirect to another host, or from https to http, does not
take the credentials along: the user name and password,
.BR \-\-oauth2\-bearer ,
.BR \-b ,
the Authorization, Proxy-Authorization and Cookie headers of
.BR \-H ,
and the request body are left out of the request that follows.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-redirs " n"
Redirect limit.  The default is 50, and \-1 is no limit.  A redirect after the
.IR n th
is exit status 47, and 0 refuses the first.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-fail
Fail the transfer when the HTTP status is 400 or above.  The body is
not written.  Without this option a 404 is a successful transfer: the
status is recorded and the body is written.
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-early
Stop at the first failed URL.  This applies to every URL on the
command line.  With
.B \-Z
the transfers that are under way are ended too, and each says
"Transfer aborted due to critical error in another transfer".
.TP
.B \-Z, \-\-parallel
Run the transfers together and not one after another, up to
.B \-\-parallel\-max
at a time.  The URLs of every
.B \-\-next
group are taken in the order they were given, as places come free.
Transfers end in any order, and bodies that go to standard output are mixed
as they arrive, so give each URL an
.B \-o
unless that is wanted.  One line of progress is drawn for the whole run (see
.BR "PROGRESS METER" ).
The exit
status is that of the most recent failure (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
Standard input goes to one transfer only: a transfer that would read it,
because of
.B "\-T \-"
or because it is a telnet URL that is given nothing to send, is refused with
"only one transfer can read standard input with \-\-parallel" when another
has it, and the command exits 2.
.B \-\-no\-parallel
undoes it.  Like
.B \-s
and
.BR \-\-stderr ,
it is not undone by
.BR \-\-next .
.TP
.BI \-\-parallel\-max " n"
With
.BR \-Z ,
run at most
.I n
transfers at once.  The default is 50.  More than 300 is 300, and 0 is the
default.  A transfer that is not yet started is not prepared, so a pattern
that makes a great many URLs does not open a great many files.  Global.
.TP
.BI \-\-parallel\-max\-host " n"
With
.BR \-Z ,
keep at most
.I n
HTTP connections open, or being made, to one host: the scheme, host and port,
and the proxy.  A transfer that would make another waits until a connection to
that host is closed or is kept for the next transfer.  Transfers that share an
HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 connection do not count as connections.  0, the default, is no
limit, and more than 65535 is 65535.  Only HTTP, HTTPS and WebSocket transfers
are held to it.  Global.
.TP
.B \-\-parallel\-immediate
With
.BR \-Z ,
start each transfer at once, making a connection of its own, rather than
waiting for a connection to the same host that is being made, to share it if the
server speaks HTTP/2 or HTTP/3.  By default a transfer waits, so a number of
URLs of one host make one connection.
.B \-\-no\-parallel\-immediate
undoes it.  Global.
.TP
.B \-\-compressed
Ask for a compressed response and decompress it: br, gzip and deflate, or gzip
and deflate when twist was built without libbrotli.
.B twist \-\-version
says
.B Features: brotli
when it was.
.TP
.BI \-\-content\-encoding " coding"
Compress the request body.
.I coding
is
.IR gzip ,
.IR deflate ,
or
.I br
when twist has libbrotli.
The request carries
.BR Content-Encoding .
Reset by
.BR \-\-next .
.TP
.B \-k, \-\-insecure
Do not check the certificate of the server for TLS, including HTTP/3: any
chain is accepted, whoever it names.  A file or directory of authorities that
the environment names and that cannot be read does not matter then.
For sftp and scp it skips the
.I known_hosts
check; a fingerprint given with
.B \-\-hostpubsha256
is still checked.
.TP
.BI \-x ", " \-\-proxy " url"
Proxy.  Use
.IR http:// ,
.I https://
or
.IR socks5:// ,
with
.I user:password@
before the host if the proxy wants them.
socks4 proxies are refused.
.B \-x ""
uses no proxy, whatever the environment says.
A proxy is also taken from the environment (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
.IP
An
.I https://
proxy is spoken to in TLS, and its certificate is checked.  The proxy is asked,
in that TLS, to CONNECT to the target: in a stream of HTTP/2 if it speaks that,
and on the connection if it speaks only HTTP/1.1, as ALPN says.  What the
target's protocol sends, including its own TLS, goes through that tunnel, so
the proxy sees where the transfer goes and how much, but not what.  Credentials
go to it as Basic, in the TLS.  This works for every scheme that can use a
proxy except ftp and ftps, and an http:// URL is tunnelled too.  A tunnel has a
connection to the proxy of its own.
.IP
HTTP/3 (\fB\-\-http3\-only\fR) goes through an
.I https://
proxy that speaks HTTP/3 and does CONNECT\-UDP (RFC 9298): QUIC to the proxy, a
CONNECT on a stream of it that asks for a UDP flow to the target, and the
target's QUIC packets in HTTP Datagrams (RFC 9297) on that stream.  The
target's TLS is still its own.  The proxy looks the target up, so its name is
not looked up here.  A proxy of another kind cannot carry HTTP/3, and then the
transfer fails, and does not go round the proxy.  A failure of the proxy's,
such as a 403 or a 407, is exit status 56.
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy\-template\-udp " template"
The URI Template (RFC 6570) of a proxy that does CONNECT\-UDP: an https:// URI
with the variables target_host and target_port, as in
.IR https://proxy.example/masque/{target_host}/{target_port}/ ,
or
.IR https://proxy.example/masque{?target_host,target_port} .
Without it the template of
.B \-x https://host:port
is
.IR https://host:port/.well\-known/masque/udp/{target_host}/{target_port}/ .
With it and no
.BR \-x ,
the template names the proxy for UDP (HTTP/3), and every other transfer is
direct.  An invalid template is a usage error (exit status 2).  Credentials
come from the proxy's URL, the template's authority, or
.BR \-U .
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy\-cacert " file"
PEM bundle of the authorities that vouch for an https proxy.  Without it the
proxy is checked against the same authorities as the target:
.BR \-\-cacert ,
.BR \-\-capath ,
the environment's (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
and the system's.
.TP
.B \-\-proxy\-insecure
Do not check the certificate of an https proxy.  This is not
.BR \-k :
that says nothing about the proxy, and the proxy is checked unless this is
given.
.TP
.BI \-\-noproxy " list"
Comma-separated hosts that do not go through a proxy, also when one is given
with
.BR \-x .
A name matches itself and the names under it:
.I local.com
matches local.com, www.local.com and not www.notlocal.com.
An address matches itself, or a network as
.IR 192.168.0.0/16 .
.B *
is every host, and the proxy is not used at all.
This overrides the environment's
.B no_proxy
and
.BR NO_PROXY ,
and
.B \-\-noproxy ""
turns them off.
.TP
.BI \-\-unix\-socket " path"
Connect to the Unix socket
.I path
in place of the host and port of the URL, for http, https, ws and wss.  The URL
still gives the Host header, the request target, and for https the name that
the certificate must have.  The host is not looked up, a redirect to another
host goes to the same socket, and no proxy is used, whether it is given with
.B \-x
or by the environment.  HTTP/3 is not possible over a socket, and a URL of
another scheme is exit status 1.  A socket that is not there is exit status 7.
.TP
.BI \-\-abstract\-unix\-socket " name"
The same for a socket in the abstract namespace of Linux.
.I name
has no NUL byte before it.  This and
.B \-\-unix\-socket
replace each other.
.TP
.B \-\-ssl
Upgrade ftp, imap, pop3 and smtp to TLS if the server offers it, and go on in
the clear if it does not.  The login comes after the upgrade.
.B \-\-ftp\-ssl
is the same.  Reset by
.BR \-\-next .
See
.BR "EXPLICIT TLS" .
.TP
.B \-\-ssl\-reqd
The same, and exit status 64 if the server will not.
.B \-\-ftp\-ssl\-reqd
is the same.  Reset by
.BR \-\-next .
.TP
.BI \-U ", " \-\-proxy\-user " user:password"
The user name and password for the proxy, if its URL has none.
.TP
.BI \-A ", " \-\-user\-agent " name"
User-Agent header.
.TP
.BI \-u ", " \-\-user " user[:password]"
User and password.  Without a colon, the password is read from
.I /dev/tty
with echo disabled, so a piped standard input is left for
.B \-d
or
.BR \-T .
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-referer " url"
Referer header.
.TP
.BI \-r ", " \-\-range " range"
Byte range.  For sftp, scp and file it is
.IR first\-last ,
.IR first\- ,
or
.IR \-count
for the last
.I count
bytes, one range only.  A range that starts beyond the end of the file exits 33.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-list\-only
List the names only, one to a line, not the server's long form, when the URL
names a directory.
For sftp.
.TP
.BI \-Q ", " \-\-quote " command"
Run an SFTP command on the server before the transfer, or after a successful
one if the command begins with
.BR \- .
A
.B *
before it lets the server refuse it.
The commands are chmod, chown, chgrp, atime, mtime, ln, symlink, mkdir, pwd,
rename, rm and rmdir.
A command that fails exits 21.
For sftp only.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit\-rate " speed"
Keep to
.I speed
bytes a second on average, for downloads and uploads.
A whole number with an optional suffix k, m, g, t or p (1024 times the one before).
For sftp and scp; other URLs are refused.
.TP
.B \-\-ftp\-create\-dirs
Make the directories an sftp upload needs.
.TP
.BI \-C ", " \-\-continue\-at " offset"
Resume at byte
.IR offset ,
for sftp and scp.
Use
.B \-
to find the offset.
A download continues from the end of the file named by
.B \-o
or
.BR \-O ,
which is opened to add to it; with a number the file is added to from that
byte of the remote file.
An upload with
.B \-T
skips that many bytes of the local file and adds to the remote one at that
offset; with
.B \-
the server is asked how big the remote file is.
Exits 36 if the remote file is shorter than the offset.
.TP
.BI \-b ", " \-\-cookie " data"
Cookie request header.  This is not a cookie jar file.
.TP
.BI \-\-oauth2\-bearer " token"
Bearer token.
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-max\-time " seconds"
Limit for the whole transfer.  Up to three decimal places are
accepted.
.TP
.BI \-\-connect\-timeout " seconds"
Limit for making the connection, in seconds; five minutes if it is not given.
For sftp and scp it covers the lookup, the TCP connect, a proxy and the SSH
handshake up to the login.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-silent
Hide the progress meter and the transfer diagnostic.  The exit status is
unchanged.
.B \-\-no\-silent
undoes it.
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-show\-error
Show the transfer diagnostic when
.B \-s
was also given, in either order.
.B \-\-no\-show\-error
undoes it.
.TP
.B \-#, \-\-progress\-bar
Show the progress as a bar in place of the meter.
.B \-\-no\-progress\-bar
undoes it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-progress\-meter
Hide the progress meter and the bar, and leave the diagnostics alone.
.B \-\-progress\-meter
undoes it.
.TP
.BI \-\-stderr " file"
Write diagnostics to \fIfile\fR.
.B \-
keeps standard error.  This applies to every URL.
.TP
.BI \-w ", " \-\-write\-out " format"
Print \fIformat\fR on standard output after the body.
.BR %{http_code} ,
.BR %{response_code} ,
.BR %{size_download} ,
.BR %{url_effective} ,
.BR %{content_type} ,
.BR %{exitcode} ,
and
.B %{errormsg}
are replaced.  \fB%{url_effective}\fR is the URL the transfer ended on, after
any redirect, and has no user name or password in it, so that a log of it holds
no secret.
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
and
.B %%
are recognised.  An unknown
.B %{name}
is empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-url " url"
A URL to fetch.
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-globoff
Do not expand {} and [] in URLs.
See
.BR "URL GLOBBING" .
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-disable
Accepted.  No config file is read.
.TP
.B \-\-next
Start a separate transfer for the following URL.  Options after it do
not inherit the previous URL's method, body, headers, output, proxy,
user, or timeouts.
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-# ,
.BR \-\-no\-progress\-meter ,
.BR \-\-stderr ,
.BR \-\-fail\-early ,
and the options of
.B \-Z
still apply.  A URL is required before
.BR \-\-next .
The transfers of the whole command line, whatever the
.B \-\-next
between them, share the connections that are kept and the names that were looked up.
.TP
.BI \-E ", " \-\-cert " file"
Client certificate in PEM form, leaf first.  Further certificates in
the file are sent after the leaf.  The matching private key is
.BR \-\-key .
The key is an unencrypted PEM file.  When a server asks for a client
certificate and none was given, the handshake fails.  When one was
given, it is presented.
.TP
.BI \-\-key " file"
Private key for
.BR \-\-cert .
For sftp and scp, the private key to log in with.
It is used alone: an ssh-agent is not asked.
Without it, the keys in the agent named by
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
are tried first, then those in
.IR ~/.ssh .
.TP
.BI \-\-pass " phrase"
Passphrase for the private key file, for the formats twist can decrypt.
A passphrase-protected key in the OpenSSH format cannot be used.
.TP
.BI \-\-hostpubsha256 " hash"
The SHA-256 of the host key of an sftp or scp server, in base64 as
.B ssh-keygen \-l
prints it, with or without
.BR SHA256: .
A host key that matches is trusted, whatever
.I known_hosts
says; one that does not is refused, even with
.BR \-k .
.TP
.BI \-\-cacert " file"
PEM trust bundle used to verify the peer.
.TP
.BI \-\-capath " directory"
A directory of certificate authorities, as PEM files whose names end in .pem,
.I .crt
or .cer, or are the eight-digit hash and number that OpenSSL's c_rehash makes.
It is used as well as
.BR \-\-cacert .
With neither, the environment's (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
and then the system's authorities are trusted.
.TP
.B \-\-http1.0
.TQ
.B \-\-http1.1
.TQ
.B \-\-http2
.TQ
.B \-\-http2-prior-knowledge
.TQ
.B \-\-http3
.TQ
.B \-\-http3-only
Select the HTTP version.  With no version selected, HTTP/3 is used
when an HTTPS record or a cached Alt-Svc entry advertises it.
Otherwise HTTPS negotiates HTTP/2 with ALPN, and cleartext offers an
h2c upgrade.  Prior knowledge is only
.BR \-\-http2-prior-knowledge .
The HTTP/1 minor version is taken from the response status line.
.TP
.BI \-\-mail-from " address"
SMTP sender.  Repeat
.B \-\-mail-rcpt
for each recipient.
.PP
.B mqtt
subscribes to the topic in the URL path, or publishes
.B \-\-data
and disconnects.
.B ldap
binds and writes the search as LDIF.  The path is the base DN.
.B ws
does the HTTP/1 upgrade, or an extended CONNECT on HTTP/2 or HTTP/3,
and writes payload bytes.  An HTTP/1 status other than 101 fails.
.B gopher
sends the URL selector and writes the reply until the server closes.
.B dict
sends one DEFINE, MATCH, or SHOW command from the URL path and writes
the reply until the server closes.
.B tftp
reads the URL path.  With
.B \-\-data
or
.B \-T
it uploads that body instead.  Lost packets are retransmitted.  A
proxy is not used.  A server error packet exits 68 through 74.
.TP
.BI \-\-dns-order " list"
Comma-separated DNS transports.  The default is
.BR doq,dot,doh,udp .
DoQ and DoT are used when the system resolver advertises them, or when
.B \-\-doq-url
or
.B \-\-dot-url
names a server.
When one of
.BR \-\-doq-url ,
.B \-\-dot-url
or
.B \-\-doh-url
names a server and this option is not given, only the named servers are asked,
in that order, and plain UDP is not: a server that is down, or that has been
made unreachable, ends the lookup and does not send the name to the system
resolver in the clear.  List
.B udp
to allow it.
UDP retries over TCP when the answer is truncated.
A host that is an IPv4 or IPv6 address is used as that address.
.TP
.BI \-\-doh-url " URL"
Resolve host names through this DNS-over-HTTPS server instead of the
transports in
.BR \-\-dns-order .
The URL must use HTTPS.  The server's name is resolved with DoQ, DoT,
or UDP, and not with DoH.  Each query is then an HTTP POST
whose body is one DNS message of type
.BR application/dns-message .
An empty URL restores the normal resolver.  When
.B \-\-dns-order
is also set, that list is used and this URL supplies the
.B doh
step.
.TP
.B \-\-doh-insecure
Do not verify the DoH server certificate.  This is independent of
.BR \-k .
.TP
.BI \-\-doq-url " URL"
DNS-over-QUIC server.
.I quic://host
or
.IR host ,
with an optional port.  The default port is 853.  The certificate is
checked against that host.  The host name is resolved with UDP.  An
empty URL clears it.  Without this option, DoQ uses the address and
name from a
.B _dns.resolver.arpa
SVCB record.  The certificate of a resolver found that way must be valid for
the name in the record and also for the address of the resolver it was found
from (RFC 9462 section 4.2), so a record that sends a lookup elsewhere is
refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-dot-url " URL"
DNS-over-TLS server.
.I tls://host
or
.IR host ,
with an optional port.  The default port is 853.  Discovery matches
.BR \-\-doq-url .
.PP
There is no config file,
.BR \-\-fail\-with\-body ,
verbose trace, or cookie jar.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The variables can be in lower case or upper case, and the lower case one has
precedence.
.B http_proxy
is the exception: it is only read in lower case, since a web server that runs a
program sets the upper case one from the request.
.PP
A proxy from the environment has the same effect as
.BR \-x ,
which overrides it.
Its value is a proxy URL as
.B \-x
takes, with
.IR http:// ,
.I https://
or
.I socks5://
before the host, and
.I user:password@
if it wants them.
An empty value is no proxy.
.TP
.B http_proxy [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for HTTP.
.TP
.B HTTPS_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for HTTPS.
.TP
.B [url-protocol]_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for the scheme of the URL, whatever it is: FTP_PROXY, FTPS_PROXY,
SFTP_PROXY, IMAP_PROXY, SMTP_PROXY, LDAP_PROXY and so on, for the schemes that
can go through a proxy.
WS_PROXY and WSS_PROXY are those of WebSocket; if they are not set the proxy
of HTTP and HTTPS is used.
.TP
.B ALL_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for a scheme that has none of its own.
.TP
.B NO_PROXY comma-separated list of hosts and domains
Hosts that do not go through a proxy.  The list is as for
.BR \-\-noproxy ,
which overrides it: a name matches itself and the names under it, an address
matches itself or a network written as 192.168.0.0/16, an IPv6 address is
written without brackets, and a single
.B *
matches all hosts.
This disables the proxy also when it was given with
.BR \-x .
.TP
.B COLUMNS terminal width
If it is a number from 21 to 9999, that many characters are the width of the
bar of
.BR \-# .
Without it the width of the terminal is used, and 79 if there is none.
.TP
.B HOME directory
Where
.I .ssh/known_hosts
is looked for, for sftp and scp.  If it is not set, the home directory in the
password file.
.TP
.B QLOGDIR directory name
Makes twist write a log of each QUIC connection (HTTP/3 and DNS over QUIC) in
that directory, in a file named after the connection ID the client chose, in
hexadecimal, and the ending
.IR .qlog .
Each line is an event of the connection, as draft-ietf-quic-qlog-quic-events
defines them, with the time in microseconds, its name and its fields as
KEY=VALUE.  The file can be read by the owner only.  A directory that is not
there means no log.  These files can become large, and say who was talked to.
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK file name
The socket of the ssh-agent that holds the keys for sftp and scp, as for ssh.
.TP
.B SSL_CERT_DIR directory
If set, it is used as the
.B \-\-capath
value.
.TP
.B SSL_CERT_FILE file name
If set, it is used as the
.B \-\-cacert
value.
.TP
.B SSLKEYLOGFILE file name
If you set this to a file name, twist writes the secrets of its TLS connections
in that file, in the NSS key log format, so that Wireshark and similar tools can
decrypt a capture of them.  This includes HTTP/3.  The file is made so that
only its owner can read it, and what is in it undoes the encryption of the
connections: it is for debugging.
.TP
.B TWIST_CA_BUNDLE file name
If set, it is used as the
.B \-\-cacert
value, and
.B SSL_CERT_DIR
and
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
are not looked at.
.PP
The names of the certificate variables are looked at only when neither
.B \-\-cacert
nor
.B \-\-capath
is given.  A file or directory that they name and that cannot be read is an
error.
.PP
A process that has more rights than the user who started it does not read the
environment at all, for any of the variables above that name a file, a
directory, a socket or a server:
.BR HOME ,
the proxy variables and
.BR NO_PROXY ,
.BR QLOGDIR ,
.BR SSH_AUTH_SOCK ,
.BR SSL_CERT_DIR ,
.BR SSL_CERT_FILE ,
.B SSLKEYLOGFILE
and
.BR TWIST_CA_BUNDLE .
That is a program that is set-user-ID or set-group-ID, one whose real and
effective IDs differ, and one that the kernel started with more rights (file
capabilities).  Whoever can set a variable for such a program can otherwise send
it to a proxy of their choosing, make it trust their certificates, or have it
write its keys to a file.  Options still work.
.PP
Twist reads no configuration file.  Variables can also be imported by
.B \-\-variable
.IR %name .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The numbers are curl's, so that a script that tests them works with either.
.PP
Several URLs leave the status of the last transfer to end.  With
.B \-Z
the transfers end in any order, and the status is that of the most recent one
that failed: a success after it does not clear it.  A transfer that was ended
because another failed (status 42) is the status only when no other transfer
has failed.  A command line that sends standard input to more than one
transfer under
.B \-Z
exits 2.
.TP
.B 0
The transfer finished.
.TP
.B 1
The scheme is not implemented.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is wrong, or twist could not initialise.
.TP
.B 3
The URL is malformed.
.TP
.B 6
The host name could not be resolved.
.TP
.B 7
The connection failed.
.TP
.B 8
The server's reply could not be used.
.TP
.B 18
The transfer ended before all of the data the server announced had come.
.TP
.B 21
A
.B \-\-quote
command of an sftp transfer failed.
.TP
.B 22
\fB\-\-fail\fP was set and the HTTP status is 400 or above.
.TP
.B 23
The output could not be written.
.TP
.B 26
A local file named by
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-\-data\-binary ,
.BR \-\-data\-urlencode ,
or
.B \-T
could not be read.
.TP
.B 27
Out of memory.
.TP
.B 25
An sftp or scp upload failed.
.TP
.B 28
The transfer timed out.
.TP
.B 33
The range cannot be delivered: it starts beyond the end of the remote file, or
is not one twist understands.
.TP
.B 36
The transfer cannot be resumed: the remote file is shorter than the offset,
the local data is, or the file to resume is not there.
.TP
.B 35
The TLS handshake failed.
.TP
.B 37
A file URL could not be opened.
.TP
.B 42
The transfer was aborted.
.TP
.B 43
A bad argument was passed to the library.
.TP
.B 47
Too many redirects.
.TP
.B 48
An option given with
.B \-t
is not one of the telnet options.
.TP
.B 49
An option given with
.B \-t
is not written as name=value, or its value is not right for it.
.TP
.B 52
The server closed before sending any data.
.TP
.B 56
A proxy refused a CONNECT or CONNECT\-UDP tunnel, with a status such as 403
or 407.
.TP
.B 61
The content encoding could not be decoded.
.TP
.B 64
TLS was asked for with
.B \-\-ssl\-reqd
and the server does not offer it or refuses it.
.TP
.B 9
The SFTP server would not let the user read the file.
.TP
.B 60
The host key of an sftp or scp server is not trusted: it is not in
.IR known_hosts ,
it differs from the one there, or it does not match
.BR \-\-hostpubsha256 .
.TP
.B 67
Authentication failed.
.TP
.B 68
TFTP file not found.
.TQ
.B 69
TFTP access violation.
.TQ
.B 70
TFTP disk full.
.TQ
.B 71
TFTP illegal operation.
.TQ
.B 72
TFTP unknown transfer.
.TQ
.B 73
TFTP file already exists.
.TQ
.B 74
TFTP no such user.
.TP
.B 78
The file does not exist on the sftp or scp server.
.TQ
.B 79
The sftp or scp server could not do what was asked.
.TP
.B 81
The operation could not be started.
.TP
.B 93
The loop was run again from inside a callback.  Only a program that uses the
library can cause this.
.TP
.B 97
A SOCKS5 proxy refused the connection or could not complete it.
.SH AUTHOR
Chris Burdess.
